  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Friday)\nIssued at 1219 PM MDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nIsolated passing thunderstorms tracked through western SD earlier\ntoday. And latest satellite and radar imagery shows clear skies\nmoving into the area. But there are hints at low-end chances for\nspotty thunderstorms again later this afternoon into evening. A\nfew boundaries are still showing up in western SD and northern\nNebraska. Also, some hi res model guidance hints at storms\nredeveloping in southwest SD this afternoon. That being said,\nthere is still some decent capping in the atmosphere but if \nstorms are able to get going, they will track northeast and are \nnot expected to be severe. Otherwise, tonight is looking like \nanother mostly dry sky night with overnight temps in the 60s.\n\nFor the weekend, afternoon highs will remain well above normal for\nearly September. Saturday's highs will be in mid to upper 90s for\nthe SD plains and low to mid 90s in NE WY. Sunday's highs will be\nin the upper 80s to mid 90s.\n\nBest precip chances on Saturday are contained to the Black Hills \nand the area to the northeast, and in central SD. SPC has placed \na marginal risk of severe storms in SD. But Sunday into Monday, \nan upper level trough looks to swing over Montana and that trough \ncould bring passing thunderstorms to the area. The SPC has placed\na marginal severe risk out for Sunday over the Black Hills and to\nthe north and machine learning severe weather models are still \nhinting at a risk of severe weather centered over the CWA Sunday\nand Monday. Stay plugged into the forecast through the weekend \nfor updates.\n\nBeyond Monday, low end chances for precip lingers in the forecast\nwith a slight cooling temperature trend.",
